# Messages.
# Format: see data/README.md. Fields:
#   sort:                    sort key (editorial, not used by the program)
#   id:                      message id, used by the program
#   error:                   title of an error message (shown in red)
#   error-revised:           revised error title (not used by the program)
#   info:                    title of an information message (shown in gold)
#   info-revised:            revised info title (not used by the program)
#   text:                    message text; \n is a line break, \l toggles symbol translation, <param> is replaced
#   description:             when the message appears (for the programmer)
#   programmer-note:         comment to the programmer
#   buttons:                 buttons: 'Label:action.Label:action;default-index'

# Instructions for the creation of this file.  Copy the Excel material and paste into this file.  [The following resizing is unnecessary.]  Pull the borders of the table to make them appropriately wide.  Save as Text.
# `s terminates the sort field, used to resort messages according to various criteria.
# `n terminates the message name or message ID
# `d terminates the description of the source for the error (explanation by the programmer)
# `p terminates a comment to the programmer.
# `e terminates the error message (displayed in red by the program)
# `E terminates a revised version of the error message
# `i terminates the information message (displayed in gold by the program)
# `I terminates a revised version of the information message
# `x terminates the explanation (explanation for the user)
# Only one of `e or `i should appear for any message
# Blanks at the start and end of strings are trimmed
# \n forces a carriage return within the text.
# \l toggles conversion of Maggie-talk.  Default is OFF.
# The parameters are: <text>, <line number>, expected <n> reference<s> for <rule name>,
# <show line number>, line <line number> is not, <stack n> is the nth WFF on the stack
# (with <stack 1> the top), <stack> is the whole stack printed vertically, <show>,
# <variable name>, no premise <premise index>, <theorem number>, <theorem name>,
# line <line number> does not precede this line, <parser error>, <branch lines left>,
# <wff>, <premises>, <rule form>, <rule form premises>, <rule form conclusion>, <remote line number>
# Sort#  `s     Name or ID  `n     Present_Message  P_M_Term     New_Message  N_M_Term     Comment to Programmer  `p     Explanation  `x     Description  `d
sort: 01
id: DerInf001
info: uniform derivation
text: When a Show line is canceled, the program notes whether it was by a "Uniform Derivation" (one in which the assumption matches the basis for boxing and canceling) or by a "Mixed Derivation" (one in which they do not).  No error is involved in either case, but the program calls it to your attention.  \n\nThis message is for information only.  Info messages appear against the usual blue background.  Error messages appear against a red background.

sort: 02
id: DerInf002
info: mixed derivation
text: When a Show line is canceled, the program notes whether it was by a "Uniform Derivation" (one in which the assumption matches the basis for boxing and canceling) or by a "Mixed Derivation" (one in which they do not).  No error is involved in either case, but the program calls it to your attention.  \n\nThis message is for information only.  Info messages appear against the usual blue background.  Error messages appear against a red background.

sort: 03
id: DerInf003
info: blank line
text: It is not strictly incorrect to include a blank line, but the program calls it to your attention.  A line can be deleted either by using the Alt+Delete key combination or by using the Toolbox that appears by right-clicking the mouse.  \n\nThis message is for information only.  Info messages appear against the usual blue background.  Error messages appear against a red background.

sort: 04
id: DerInf004
info: nothing to check
text: The WFF field is blank.\n\nThis message is generated by a Check, and will appear even if the Justification field contains a putative justification.  \n\nIf there is something in the Justification field, and Command Mode is enabled, you may regard the justification as a command, and attempt to execute it by placing the cursor in the Justification field, and pressing Shift+Enter.  The command will then be executed, or, if there is an error in the justification, a different message will appear.
description: This INFO message is generated by a Check.  Note that this message will appear even if there is a justification, including a fallacious justification that would generate a different error message in Command Mode (e.g. when referencing a line with a parse error).

sort: 05
id: DerInf005
info: Correct Derivation
text: Congratulations!

sort: 06

sort: 07
id: DerErrTxt
error: <text>
text: This explanation is intended to serve for many different messages.\n\nMessages of the form "can not map xxx to yyy" indicate that the substitution you proposed in answer to a query was of the wrong grammatical type.  For example, if you answered a query for an instantial term with "P", such a message would be generated because "P" is a sentence letter and not a term.  Similarly, if you were introducing an instance of a theorem, and answered a query for a substitution instance of "Q" with "x", the message "can not map Q to x" would be generated because "x" is a variable, and cannot replace a sentence letter.\n\nMessages of the form "parse error at token n" indicate that the expression in the WFF field is not well-formed.  The parser believes that the first error occurs at about the nth symbol.\n\nMessages of the form "lexical error at token n" indicate that the expression in the WFF field contains a symbol that is not in the vocabulary of the language.  The parser believes that the first such occurs at about the nth symbol.
description: I added this for some auto-generated messages that should probably have entries elsewhere in this database.

sort: 08
id: DerErr001
error: syntax error in Justification: incomplete assumption
text: You appear to be attempting to enter an Assumption line.  But you have omitted the rule name.  There are two justifications for Assumption lines: \n\n<indent 5>ASS CD \n(the assumption for conditional derivation), and \n\n<indent 5>ASS ID \n(the assumption for indirect derivation).

