text: Do *you* see something in the Justification field?

sort: 57
id: DerErr050
error: one justification per line
text: You appear to have attempted to use Justification Queuing, but Justification Queuing is not enabled for this problem.

sort: 58
id: DerErr051
error: not subordinate to any show line
text: This message is posted for lines that are not subordinate to any Show line, and are not themselves Show lines.  Note that the reverse highlight, which appears when your cursor is in the line, appears on the Problem Line, and not on any Show line within the derivation.  \n\nLines that are not subordinate to any Show line will, of course, never be boxed, and lines that are not Show lines will never contain canceled Show.  Thus the lines flagged by this message will prevent the derivation from ever being technically "complete", i.e. from ever being one in which every line is either in a box or contains a canceled "Show".  The importance of a derivation being complete first appears in Chapter 3, wherein it is possible to give a derivation, albeit incomplete, for a fallacious argument.  \n\nLines for which this message is posted should either be deleted (Alt+Delete), indented (Alt+[Right Arrow]), or become Show lines (Ctrl+Shift+S).

sort: 59
id: DerErr052
error: no problem given
text: This message appears on the Problem Line.  Your derivation cannot be correct if there is no argument to derive.

sort: 60
id: DerErr053
error: problem has no conclusion
text: The conclusion must follow the 3-dot pyramid that is sometimes called the "therefore" sign.  You can enter this sign using Ctrl+Shift+T or by right-clicking on the Problem Line, and then using the tool-bar.

sort: 61
id: DerErr054
error: Conclusion not being shown
text: In addition to providing Information and flagging Errors on each line, the Checking process provides a summary judgment for the entire derivation.  These summary judgments appear on the Problem Line.  They are:\n\nCorrect Derivation\n\nConclusion not being shown\n\nIncomplete Derivation\n\nIncorrect Derivation\n\nThese messages are arranged in a hierarchy, from the top down, with the first applicable one being posted.  The judgment "Conclusion not being shown" is posted whenever the conclusion of the argument does not appear in a top level Show line (it might appear in a subderivation).  A derivation might be correct in all other respects, but still fail to show the conclusion of the argument on the Problem Line, in which case this message would appear.\n\nThe Error message "Checking canceled" will appear if a query posted during the checking process is canceled without an answer.

sort: 62
id: DerErr055
error: not yet canceled
text: Does the "Show" *look* canceled?

sort: 63
id: DerErr056
error: checking canceled
text: You appear to have canceled out of a query during checking.  The program cannot complete the checking process without an answer to the query.  If you wish, you may initiate checking again, and answer the query.

sort: 64
id: DerErr057
error: Incorrect Derivation
text: In addition to providing Information and flagging Errors on each line, the Checking process provides a summary judgment for the entire derivation.  These summary judgments appear on the Problem Line.  They are:\n\nCorrect Derivation\n\nConclusion not being shown\n\nIncomplete Derivation\n\nIncorrect Derivation\n\nThese messages are arranged in a hierarchy, from the top down, with the first applicable one being posted.  Thus, the judgment "Incorrect" implies that the conclusion does appears in an appropriate Show line, and that the derivation is "complete", but that it contains one or more errors.\n\nThe Error message "Checking canceled" will appear if a query posted during the checking process is canceled without an answer.

sort: 65
id: DerErr058
error: Incomplete Derivation
text: In addition to providing Information and flagging Errors on each line, the Checking process provides a summary judgment for the entire derivation.  These summary judgments appear on the Problem Line.  They are:\n\nCorrect Derivation\n\nConclusion not being shown\n\nIncomplete Derivation\n\nIncorrect Derivation\n\nThese messages are arranged in a hierarchy, from the top down, with the first applicable one being posted.  \n\nThe judgment "Incomplete" implies that the conclusion does appears in an appropriate Show line, but that the derivation contains lines that are neither in a box nor contain a canceled "Show".  Although there are some subtle cases in which the preceding definition must be applied exactly, generally, derivations marked "Incomplete" are obviously incomplete.  They should be completed.  \n\nThe importance of a derivation being technically complete first appears in Chapter 3, wherein it is possible to give a derivation, albeit technically incomplete, in which the conclusion of a fallacious argument appears in a canceled Show line.\n\nThe Error message "Checking canceled" will appear if a query posted during the checking process is canceled without an answer.

sort: 66
id: DerErr059
error: parse error: \l<parser error>
text: The parser believes that \n     \l<parser error>\l \nis neither a term nor a formula, thus not a well formed expression at all.  It may be right or it may be wrong.\n\nIf you think it is wrong, please send a Suggestion Box message including a copy of what is in this message.  \n\nTo copy this message, click anywhere within it, press Ctrl+a to highlight it, and press Ctrl+c to copy it.  Paste it into your Suggestion Box message by pressing Ctrl+v.  (You will have to close this window before opening the Suggestion box.)  If you wish a personal response, add your e-mail address to your message.  \n\nIn order to preserve the form through e-mail translations, the ASCII version of the expression is also included\n     <parser error>
description:

