id: DerNot024
text: An occurrence of the instantial variable "<inst var>" \nwill be captured by a quantifier in the original \nformula. \n\nIf we replace all free occurrences of "<gen var>" in\n<indent 5><gen wff>\nwith the instantial variable "<inst var>", we obtain\n<indent 5><inst wff>,\nin which one of the *new* occurrences of "<inst var>" in \n<indent 5><op wfe>\nhas been captured by the quantifier phrase "<op phrase>".\n\nIn answer to the request to specify an instantial \nvariable to replace "<gen var>", you must specify a variable that \nwill not be captured by a quantifier that already \nexists in \n<indent 5><gen wff>\n
description: Used for EI where occurrences of the inst term get captured by pre-existing quantifiers in the gen wff

sort: 81n
id: DerNot025
text: The variable you have chosen produces a "clash" \nwith free variables in the original formula.\n\nIf we replace the selected occurrences of "<inst term>" in\n<indent 5><inst wff>\nwith occurrences of "<gen var>", we obtain\n<indent 5><gen wff>.\nwhen we then generalize on "<gen var>",\n<indent 3><gen all>\nthe existential quantifier will capture a pre-existing \nfree occurrence of "<gen var>" that was free in \n<indent 5><inst wff>\nand was *not* selected (and so should remain free).\n
description: NEW  Used for EG where the new quantifier unintentionally captures some free variables that should have remained free after the EG.

